unit-513 post-hoc 6 — line-level reconciliation: did the arm MISS sites, or were they APPENDED?

## §0 selector provenance
  byte-identical to the arm's selector line: YES

## §1 blob reader — known positives FIRST
  PASS  arm blob at 2473f854 readable and non-empty (20126 B)
  PASS  selector returns non-zero on that blob (4) — an empty-returning reader would call every site APPENDED
  PASS  absent path returns null rather than empty text

BLOB READER KNOWN POSITIVES: 3 of 3

## §2 partition of the 44 HEAD sites
  HEAD sites                                   44
  NEW-FILE   (file postdates the arm)          17
  PRESENT-THEN (matchable in the arm blob)     27
  APPENDED   (old file, pattern not then)      0

  Sites the arm's key WOULD have returned over the files HEAD sites live in: 27

## §3 the APPENDED sites, printed in full
  none.

## §4 verdict
  Published at the arm commit: 23 sites.
  PRESENT-THEN at HEAD:        27
  delta: +4

  THE ARM UNDER-COUNTED. 4 site(s) were matchable by the arm's OWN key in files
  that existed at its own commit, and are not in the published 23. Post-hoc 4's "different
  commits" prose is REFUTED as the explanation for that part of the gap.

## §5 limits of THIS rung, stated before anyone cites it
  - Starts from HEAD, so a site that existed at the arm commit in a file since DELETED or
    RENAMED is invisible here. That is the one remaining direction and it is NOT driven.
  - Matches on (file, pattern) multiset counts, not line identity (see the header): a pattern
    duplicated within a file is reconciled by count, so the arm's own four identical fixture
    patterns are indistinguishable from each other.
